            "status": "succeeded",
            "output": "Review: This function is wrong because of operator precedence: `/` binds tighter than `-`, so it returns the mean minus 1 instead of the mean. It also raises ZeroDivisionError when given an empty list.\n\nCAROL SAYS: Corrected:\n\n    def mean(xs): return sum(xs) / len(xs)\n\nThe bug: division binds tighter than subtraction, so the original computes (sum(xs) / len(xs)) - 1, which is the mean minus 1. The arithmetic mean has no \"- 1\" term. If you meant to divide by (n - 1), as in a sample-variance denominator, that would be sum(xs) / (len(xs) - 1), but that isn't a mean.\n\nOptional guard for empty input:\n\n    def mean(xs): return sum(xs) / len(xs) if xs else float('nan')"
